{"context":"- Anvil v2 distribution is registry-driven: delivery lifecycle standards are independent repositories, discovered and installed through the standard registry (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3 A4–A6).\n- Distribution starts first-party (maleolabs namespace); community standards are future repositories (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §4.1, §4.4).\n- Community enablement is gated: opening the registry to community standards is post-gate work in Phase 7, bound to the Review 20 §2.4 outcomes (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §8 ADR-034 row, §10 Phase 7).\n- Third-party participation must be structurally possible without any Core change — success criterion 9 (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §13). Source: ADR-034-community-standard-contribution-policy.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"- A community contribution policy defines: **namespace, acceptance criteria (contract conformance, all seven parts of the standard structure, verification coverage, tests, maintainership commitment), and review path** (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §4.4).\n- Community standards pass the **SAME registry validation as first-party standards; no privileged distribution path** (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §4.4).\n- **Gating:** community enablement is post-gate work (Phase 7), bound to the Review 20 §2.4 outcomes — working end-to-end lifecycle, demonstrated convention depth, and one external consumer. Registry-driven FIRST-PARTY distribution is v2 scope; opening the registry to community standards is not.\n- First-party standards remain maintainable by the core team regardless of community participation (A","consequences":"- **Positive:** a structural, gated path for third-party participation with the same validation as first-party standards.\n- **Positive:** risk mitigation for the no-maintainer and contract-divergence risks (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §11.4).\n- **Negative:** the community phase may never open if the gate fails — the plan requires stopping rather than drifting (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §10 Phase 7, §11.3).\n- **Neutral:** policy authoring is Phase 7 work (EPIC-020), not v2 launch work.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Open the registry to community standards at v2 launch.** Rejected: community enablement is explicitly gated on Review 20 §2.4 outcomes (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §8 ADR-034 \"P2 (gated)\", §10 Phase 7); the community phase does not open on gate failure.\n- **A separate, lighter validation path for community standards.** Rejected: community standards pass the same registry validation as first-party; no privileged distribution path (§4.4).\n- **No community contribution path.** Rejected"}